ddiugstone, Midhurst, Penn, and many nowheres, and now Aylesbury.", "reference": "1912, Charles Inge, “Nemesis or Bad Luck?”, in The Windsor magazine, volume 36, page 95:"}, {"text": "On a warm summer's evening On a train bound for nowhere I met up with the gambler", "reference": "1976, “The Gambler”, Don Schlitz (lyrics):"}, {"text": "Oh, not the middle of nowhere like the rest of Indiana, but a nowhere so flat and ugly you want to lie down in a ditch and never get up again.", "reference": "1996 Oct, Indianapolis Monthly, volume 20, number 2, page 115:"}, {"text": "But some Nowheres do still exist and are there to be found by any genuinely free spirit willing to hook a caravan behind his four-wheel-drive and dream, say, of finding that isolated campsite beside an as yet undiscovered waterhole", "reference": "2005, Dave Finkelstein with Jack London and Philip Caputo, Greater Nowheres: Wanderings Across the Outback, page xxiv:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nowhere", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}